# Google Play moderation hardening — anti-fingerprint invariants

> **Read this before every submission.** It is the Android counterpart of the
> iOS `apple_moderation_hardening.mdc`, rewritten around how Google actually
> enforces: Play Protect static scanning, the Play Console policy engine, and
> the account-level graph that links developer accounts to each other.
>
> Cross-references: `kotlin_fingerprint.mdc` (the mechanism), `START_HERE.md`
> (the three-command setup), `FINAL_CHECKLIST.md` (the ship gate).

## 0. How Google differs from Apple — and where it doesn't

Apple kills apps *before* review with a static pass over the IPA plus a graph
query on the developer account. Google is less aggressive pre-publication but
far more aggressive **after**: a policy strike on one app propagates to every
app under the same developer account, and Google links accounts by payment
instrument, D-U-N-S, upload IP, signing key, and the API keys embedded in the
APKs themselves.

The practical consequence is the same. **The fingerprint that matters is not
the one a reviewer sees on day one; it is the one that links app #7 to app #2
after app #2 gets a strike.** Everything below is about making that link not
exist.

What is different on Android and worth knowing:

- **The signing key is a hard join.** Two apps signed with the same upload key
  are the same developer to Google, full stop. Play App Signing does not hide
  this — the upload certificate is recorded.
- **`applicationId` collisions and near-collisions** (`com.acme.game1`,
  `com.acme.game2`) are a trivially queryable pattern. Vary the reverse-domain
  root, not just the leaf.
- **APK static analysis is string-heavy.** Google's scanners index strings and
  class names in the DEX far more than Apple indexes Mach-O symbols. R8
  renames your classes but does not touch string constants, resource names, or
  anything reachable by reflection — which is exactly why the fingerprint
  engine exists.
- **`google-services.json` is a loud join.** It carries the Firebase project
  id, project number, and API key in the clear inside the APK. Two apps
  sharing a Firebase project share an unambiguous identifier.

## 1. What the fingerprint engine already handles

Do not re-do any of this by hand. `gray.seed` → `app/build.gradle.kts` →
`BuildConfig` covers:

| Category | Values |
|---|---|
| Storage | plain + encrypted prefs filenames, every key inside |
| JS | safe-area sentinel, keyboard sentinel, JS bridge class name |
| Push | FCM channel id, channel title |
| Crypto | codec seed bytes, multiplier, offset, algorithm variant |
| Timings | push snooze, all attribution/config/GCD timeouts, connectivity grace, safe-area delay, heartbeat, redirect budget |
| Network identity | Chrome major/build/patch in the User-Agent |
| Secrets | config endpoint, AppsFlyer key, Firebase project number, GCD base — encoded at build time |

`tools/rebrand.py` covers package name, sub-package folders, class names and
the drawable prefix.

**Verify with `gradlew grayReport` and diff against the previous project.**
If any line matches a sibling, the seed was reused.

## 2. What the engine cannot do — the operator's list

These are the joins that live outside the source tree. Every one of them has
sunk a portfolio.

### 2a. Hard joins (Google links accounts on these directly)

- **Upload signing key.** One keystore per app. Never reuse a `.jks` across
  submissions, never reuse the key alias, never reuse the password.
- **Firebase project.** One project per app. A shared `google-services.json`
  puts the same `project_number` and `mobilesdk_app_id` in two APKs.
- **AppsFlyer dev key.** One key per app where the account allows it. The key
  is encoded in the APK, but it decodes to the same plaintext once one
  submission is unpacked.
- **Play Console developer account.** One portfolio slot per account, with
  independent payment instruments and legal entities. Sharing an account means
  a strike on one is a strike on all.
- **Config endpoint domain.** Different registrar, different registration
  date, different nameservers, different hosting IP. A `whois` sweep across
  sibling domains showing the same registrar and same-week registration is
  trivially clustered.

### 2b. Soft joins (individually weak, together decisive)

- Upload IP / CI machine. Rotate, or at minimum do not upload two apps from
  the same office IP within a short window.
- Internal testing tester emails. Different set per app; no shared personal
  addresses.
- Store listing text. Do not reuse phrasing, screenshot templates, feature
  graphic layouts, or the same stock art pack.
- Privacy policy URL and its hosting. A shared policy page is a direct link.
- Package reverse-domain root. Vary it: `com.<different-brand>.<word>`.
- Release cadence. Seven apps published in one week from seven accounts with
  the same structure is a pattern in itself.

## 3. Policy surfaces specific to the gray flow

### 3a. The two-faced app (Deceptive Behavior / Device and Network Abuse)

A single remote boolean selecting between a native game and a full-screen
WebView is the structural marker. It cannot be removed without removing the
product, but the signal can be lowered:

- **Keep the native part real and reviewable.** The white part is what passes
  review. A placeholder Activity with a centered label is an automatic
  Deceptive Behavior finding. `NativeContentActivity` in this template is a
  stub — it **must** be replaced before submission.
- **Keep the native part reachable from the gray path.** A "Play offline"
  entry in the WebView shell that navigates to the game removes the
  "unreachable subtree" signal from a reachability analysis.
- **Do not ship large dead weight in the unpicked branch.** If the game bundle
  is more than ~3× the gray shell, either shrink it or move it to Play Asset
  Delivery.

### 3b. The post-review URL router (Deceptive Behavior 4.1 / Unwanted Software)

The app can be handed a URL by the config endpoint or by a push payload. Left
ungated, that is "the app changes its purpose after review".

- **`UrlGuard` must be armed.** `gray.allowedHosts` empty means every incoming
  URL is accepted. The build prints a warning; do not ship past it.
- **Never allowlist a wildcard TLD** (`com`, `net`). Allowlist the specific
  partner hosts.
- **The NATIVE lock must hold.** Once an install is NATIVE, no push and no
  config answer may flip it to a WebView. Enforced in `WelcomePortal` and
  `PushRelay` — do not "temporarily" relax it for a test.
- **Saved URLs expire.** `DataVault.isUrlValid()` honours the `expires` field
  from the server. Do not persist a URL with no expiry when the server sends
  one.

### 3c. Permissions — declaration must match use

Google's pre-launch report flags declared permissions with no matching call.
The manifest currently declares four:

| Permission | Justification | Remove if… |
|---|---|---|
| `INTERNET` | everything | never |
| `ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE` | `NetWire` connectivity gate | never |
| `POST_NOTIFICATIONS` | FCM prompt on API 33+ | the app ships without push |
| `VIBRATE` | notification channel vibration | you disable `enableVibration` |

**Never add** `QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES`, `REQUEST_INSTALL_PACKAGES`,
`SYSTEM_ALERT_WINDOW`, `READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE`, or any location permission.
Each one moves the app into a policy category with a declaration form
attached, and the gray flow needs none of them. The WebView file chooser works
through `ActivityResultContracts` and needs no storage permission — see
pitfalls #12.

### 3d. Data safety form

The form must match what the APK actually does. The gray flow collects:

- **Device or other IDs** — AppsFlyer ID, FCM token. Purpose: Analytics,
  Advertising. Shared with third parties: yes (AppsFlyer, Firebase).
- **App activity** — none beyond attribution unless the partner site is
  declared separately.

Declaring "no data collected" while shipping the AppsFlyer SDK is a
straightforward policy violation and it is checked automatically.

### 3e. Metadata coherence (Play policy: Misrepresentation)

`settings.gradle.kts` `rootProject.name`, `gray.appLabel`, the Play listing
title, the screenshots and the description must all describe the same native
game. A listing describing a puzzle game over an APK whose only real screen
is a WebView is the clearest possible finding.

Check before every upload:

```powershell
rg -n 'rootProject.name' settings.gradle.kts
rg -n '^gray\.appLabel' gray.properties
rg -n 'app_name' app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml
```

## 4. Release hygiene the scanner reads

- **R8 on, resource shrinking on.** Already configured. Verify the mapping
  file is uploaded to Play so crash reports remain readable.
- **`Trace` compiles out.** Every gray-flow log goes through `core/Trace`,
  guarded by `BuildConfig.DEBUG`. A release build with `Sending first launch`
  or a dumped config body in logcat is both a leak and a marker.
- **`gray.debugForceUrl` empty in release.** The build script forces it; do
  not add a second path.
- **No `applicationIdSuffix`.** Breaks attribution (pitfalls #1) and produces
  a second package id in the Firebase project.
- **`allowBackup=false` + `data_extraction_rules.xml`.** Already set. Stops an
  old server answer travelling to a new device and stops the encrypted prefs
  file being an inter-device join.
- **`android:debuggable` never set true in release.** AGP handles this; do not
  override it.

## 5. Verification commands

Run all of these before every upload. Every one must return zero matches
inside `app/src/main/java/` unless noted.

```powershell
# 5.1 — Fingerprint literals that must live in BuildConfig, not source.
rg -n '259_?200|Chrome/1[0-9][0-9]\.|__flsa\b|__kb\b|KbPan|fl_state|fl_enc|app_push_channel|ChangeThisSeed' app/src/main/java

# 5.2 — The template package must be gone after rebrand.py.
rg -n 'com\.example\.grayshell' app/src/main/java app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml app/proguard-rules.pro

# 5.3 — Logging outside the Trace bottleneck.
rg -n 'Log\.(i|w|e|d|v)\(' app/src/main/java | rg -v 'core[/\\]Trace\.kt'

# 5.4 — Permissions: nothing beyond the four justified ones.
rg -n 'uses-permission' app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml

# 5.5 — Allowlist armed (must be NON-empty).
rg -n '^gray\.allowedHosts' gray.properties

# 5.6 — Debug URL empty (must be blank or absent).
rg -n '^gray\.debugForceUrl' gray.properties

# 5.7 — White part is real, not the stub.
rg -n 'Replace .*with your real game|NATIVE CONTENT \(white part\)' app/src/main/java

# 5.8 — Seed is not the template default.
rg -n 'CHANGE-ME-EVERY-PROJECT' gray.properties

# 5.9 — Derived fingerprint, for diffing against the previous project.
gradlew grayReport
```

`5.1`–`5.3`, `5.7`, `5.8` must be empty. `5.5` must be non-empty. `5.4` must
list exactly the four permissions from §3c. `5.6` must be blank.

## 6. The one-line summary

The template makes it impossible to accidentally ship two apps with the same
binary fingerprint. It cannot make it impossible to ship two apps with the
same **keystore, Firebase project, AppsFlyer key, domain registrar and
developer account** — and those are the joins that actually terminate
portfolios. §2a is not optional.
